 ## Background
-The concept of single scattering was first introduced by Kajiya [KH84] as a two-pass method for rendering participating media, marking the first model to account for scattering [S1, unofficial]. Max [Max86] extended Blinn's layered medium model to arbitrary shapes using analytical functions, and later [Max94] added anisotropic light dispersion to account for effects like halos [S1, unofficial]. The approximation remains standard for real-time rendering and atmospheric effects due to its efficiency, though it is limited to low-albedo media [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of single scattering was first introduced by Kajiya [KH84] as the first model allowing for scattering rendering, using a two-pass approach [S1, unofficial]. Max [Max86] extended Blinn's layer-based model to arbitrary shapes using analytical functions, and later [Max94] added anisotropic light dispersion [S1, unofficial]. Blinn's original model was limited to layer-shaped media [S1, unofficial]. Modern implementations often use Monte Carlo methods or deterministic ray marching with precomputed shadowing for real-time performance [S1, unofficial].
 
